+*   **Performance:** Achieves state-of-the-art HDR and deblurring novel view synthesis results on synthetic and real datasets compared to EvHDR-NeRF and HDR-NeRF [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Physically consistent 3D representation; handles single-exposure blurry LDR images and events; explicitly models sensor physics for both RGB and event cameras.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires corresponding event data; relies on accurate pose estimation (event-guided COLMAP); joint optimization of multiple networks may be computationally intensive.
+*   **When to use:** When reconstructing sharp HDR scenes from single-exposure blurry images and event streams, particularly in extreme lighting conditions.
+*   **When not to use:** When only standard LDR images are available without events, or when real-time performance is critical and the overhead of joint CRF optimization is prohibitive.
 
 ## Background
-See-NeRF was proposed by researchers from iCVTEAM (Institute of Computer Vision and Technology) [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of previous methods like EvHDR-NeRF, which extended HDR-NeRF by applying tone mapping directly to 3D points without considering event generation characteristics [S1, unofficial]. See-NeRF explicitly models the radiance-to-measurement process for both RGB and event sensors, jointly optimizing the NeRF network with differentiable CRF models [S1, unofficial]. The code and datasets are publicly available at https://github.com/iCVTEAM/See-NeRF [S1, unofficial].
+See-NeRF was proposed by researchers from iCVTEAM. It addresses the limitations of prior works like EvHDR-NeRF, which extended HDR-NeRF by applying tone mapping to 3D points without considering event generation characteristics [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on the physical imaging principles of RGB and event sensors, leveraging spatial and temporal dynamic information in events to enhance sharp HDR 3D representation learning [S1, unofficial].
 
